# ops/local/Caddyfile — committed, repo-friendly version of the local
# Caddyfile used by the Docker + Caddy + SSH-tunnel development stack
# documented in `docs/LOCAL-DEVELOPMENT.md`.
#
# RUNTIME USAGE (operator runs on their dev VM):
#    mkdir -p ~/instaedit-certs
#    cp ops/local/Caddyfile ~/Caddyfile
#
#    docker run -d \
#      --name instaedit-caddy \
#      --restart unless-stopped \
#      --network host \
#      -v ~/Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile:ro \
#      -v ~/instaedit-certs:/certs:ro \
#      caddy:2
#
# Once the certs in ~/instaedit-certs/ come from `mkcert` on the operator's
# machine (scp'd here), Chrome trusts the localhost:8443 origin and the
# browser can hit Vite (via this Caddy) at the same single-origin as the
# Go API — cookies stay host-only, SessionStorage + refresh cookies work.
#
# ── ARCHITECTURE NOTE — locked-in routing intent ─────────────────────
# * PRIMARY APP is InstaEdit Social (Vite on 127.0.0.1:5173, SPA
#   fallback handler at the bottom of this site block). The browser
#   enters at `/` and navigates to /groups/:groupId/videos, /login, etc.
# * SECONDARY APP is InstaEditor (Next.js on 127.0.0.1:3001) and it
#   is reachable EXCLUSIVELY under the Next basePath `/dark_editor_v2/*`.
#   Next emits its `_next/*` assets under the basePath automatically, so
#   they are intercepted by `handle /dark_editor_v2/*` below. There is
#   INTENTIONALLY NO root-level `handle /_next*` handler here — adding one
#   would leak the editor's static assets out of the /dark_editor_v2
#   namespace and is forbidden.
# * `/api/*` is the Go API (127.0.0.1:8080 — the container port; with
#   docker-compose.local.yml's overlay binding 127.0.0.1:8081:8080 also
#   reachable, but 8080 is canonical because it matches docker-compose.yml).
# * `/internal/*` is INTENTIONALLY ABORTED in this block — those routes
#   are auth-gated on the API container and only reachable from the
#   `media-backend` docker network (see docker-compose.yml). This is the
#   VPS hardening mirrored locally.
# * AUTHORIZATION IS NOT PERFORMED BY CADDY. Caddy is pure routing.
#   Authorization lives in:
#     (a) Go auth/CSRF middleware on `/api/v1/*`, and
#     (b) the InstaEditor session-loader which MUST call
#         GET /api/v1/youtube/editor-sessions/by-project/{projectId}
#         BEFORE mounting the Canvas. A direct hit to
#         /dark_editor_v2/editor/{unknown-id} returns 404 from the
#         backend, the gate refuses to mount, and the page redirects
#         back to /groups or /dashboard-channels.
# * TEST ENTRYPOINTS MUST start from `/` (InstaEdit Social) →
#   `/groups/:groupId/videos` → click "Apri InstaEditor" → use the
#   `editor_url` returned by POST /api/v1/youtube/editor-sessions.
#   Never short-circuit straight to /dark_editor_v2/editor/<fabricated>
#   in E2E tests.

{
    admin off
    auto_https off
}

# `handle` (not `handle_path`) so Go's router sees the FULL path
# including `/api` — the API mounts under `/api/v1/...` and a stripped
# prefix would 404 every request.
#
# Hostname-less site block (`https://:8443`) on purpose: when reached
# directly from a browser, the Host header is `localhost`; when reached
# via `cloudflared --url https://localhost:8443`, cloudflared forwards
# the public `Host` (e.g. `village-laura-…trycloudflare.com`). Pinning
# the block to `https://localhost:8443` would produce a 0-byte response
# for the cloudflared path because Caddy would not match the site. Any
# Host header is acceptable here because this single-origin proxy is
# only used in development with both Vite and Go on the same machine.
#
# The mkcert cert SAN is only `localhost`. The cloudflared→Caddy hop
# skips cert validation via `--no-tls-verify`, so the SAN mismatch on
# the public Host is harmless. Direct browser access to the tunnel
# URL+port will warn — use the cloudflared leg or stay on localhost.
https://:8443 {
    tls /certs/localhost.pem /certs/localhost-key.pem

    # Velox internal endpoints (service-to-service only). These
    # routes are NOT supposed to resolve on the local-dev origin;
    # they live on the InstaEdit API but the host-published PORT
    # 8080 should NEVER be reverse-proxied for them — Velox talks
    # to /internal/v1/* directly over the `media-backend` docker
    # network (see docker-compose.yml / docker-compose.local.yml).
    # The explicit `abort` here mirrors the VPS hardening so the
    # dev tunnel (cloudflared → :8443 → api) also aborts if anyone
    # ever tries to probe internal paths externally.
    handle /internal/* {
        abort
    }

    handle /api/* {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
    }

    # InstaEditor: applicazione Next.js secondaria, accessibile solo sotto /dark_editor_v2
    # Next.js ha basePath: '/dark_editor_v2', quindi anche gli asset /_next/* sono serviti sotto questo path
    handle /dark_editor_v2/* {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3001
    }

    # Dev S3 proxy: path-style presigned uploads land at
    # /{bucket}/{key} (bucket = instaedit-local). Caddy forwards to the
    # host-published MinIO port; the original Host is preserved so the
    # SigV4 `host` header MinIO validates matches the signed URL.
    handle /instaedit-local/* {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:19000 {
            header_up Host {host}
        }
    }

    handle {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
    }
}
